<section class="reference-section" id="matsya-flood"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Matsya Avatara · The Great Cosmic Flood</h2><a href="/matsya-flood.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Matsya Mahapurana · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition · Adi Khanda, chapters 1–2.</strong> This original condensed English retelling is based on the owner-supplied scan archive. The website does not distribute the book file or reproduce its Hindi explanation.</p><ol><li><strong>1.8–17 · printed pp. 13–14</strong><small>Manu’s austerity, the boon of preserving living beings and the fish entering his hands.</small></li><li><strong>1.18–29 · printed pp. 15–16</strong><small>The fish outgrows the pot, larger vessel, well, lake and ocean; Manu recognizes Vishnu.</small></li><li><strong>1.30–35 · printed pp. 15–16</strong><small>The approaching dissolution, the divine boat and the instruction to preserve the forms and sources of life.</small></li><li><strong>2.1–16 · printed pp. 16–17</strong><small>Manu’s questions and Matsya’s account of the deluge and cosmic dissolution.</small></li><li><strong>2.17–21 · printed p. 17</strong><small>Matsya’s one horn, the serpent-rope, the boat, the seven sages and the narration of the Purana during the crossing.</small></li><li><strong>2.22–24 · printed pp. 18–19</strong><small>The range of teachings requested by Manu.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Naming note:</strong> the supplied edition calls the king Manu and says in 1.34–35 that he will become Vaivasvata Manu. Its Hindi note distinguishes the Bhagavata’s familiar name Satyavrata. The story page therefore uses “Manu · Satyavrata” as a cross-tradition guide while identifying the wording of the source followed.</p><p>Chapter 2.10–16 places the Vedas, Puranas and branches of learning within the preservation framework; 2.20–21 presents Matsya’s narration to Manu and the sages. The reader condenses the source’s detailed dissolution imagery and later catalogue of topics. Four commissioned images are AI-generated devotional interpretations; no illustration is presented as a historical reconstruction. The pause-and-reflect question is editorial.</p><a href="/mahapuranas.html#matsya">Meet the Matsya Purana</a></div></section>
